  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 121 AM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nRelatively quiet weather is expected across South Texas through the \nweekend as weak mid-level ridging, limited moisture, and a lack of \nappreciable forcing keep rain chances low. A few isolated to \nscattered remain possible, mainly through much of next week. \nOtherwise, east to southeast low-level flow will persist.\n\nThe upper-level pattern becomes more unsettled early next week as \nridging shifts east and troughing develops across the central CONUS. \nLatest model guidance has backed off on bringing a cold front \nthrough South Texas, with the boundary now expected to remain north \nof the CWA. Nevertheless, increasing moisture and passing \ndisturbances aloft will support low to moderate chances for showers \nand thunderstorms beginning Monday or Tuesday and continuing through \nmuch of next week. With stronger forcing remaining north of the \nregion, widespread rainfall appears unlikely, though uncertainty \nremains regarding precipitation timing and coverage.\n\nTemperatures will remain near seasonal normals for highs and \nslightly above normal for lows. With no significant frontal passage \nanticipated, little change in temperatures is expected through the \nend of the period. \n\nGuidance continues to suggest elevated tides continue this weekend \naccording to P-ETSS. Leading to the potential for minor coastal \nflooding into early next week. Astronomical tides combined with \nonshore flow around 15 knots will increase the risk of rip currents \nthis weekend, so have a moderate rip current risk through at least \nSaturday. Please use caution if going to the beach.",
